  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061n=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061q=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians.",
  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={\u2026,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\u2026}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"",
